orecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Colorado has voted Democratic in every presidential election since 2004, and its partisan lean sits roughly six points in the Democratic column. With a term-limited incumbent leaving the White House, both parties will nominate fresh candidates, and neither side enjoys an incumbency advantage. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals.\n\nEarly signals from the 2026 midterms show warning signs for Republicans among women voters, while Colorado Democrats absorbed progressive primary challenges that could shape the party's eventual nominee. On the Republican side, early 2028 positioning features figures such as Rubio and Vance. Until nominees and real polling emerge, the state's underlying lean is the best guide, pointing to a modest but meaningful Democratic advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7597, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6966, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7137, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.701, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.713, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7202, "margin": 6.0}]}